        "bodyTr": "The United States is maintaining its position that conditions remain unmet regarding verification of full operational capability (FOC) necessary for South Korea to assume wartime operational control (OPCON). The government had envisioned completing verification this year and establishing a target date for transition, but it now faces an impasse at the first hurdle due to diverging views between Seoul and Washington. General Xavier Brunson, commander of U.S. Forces Korea, warned on the 12th during a discussion at the Center for Strategic and International Studies that \"time is also a condition,\" cautioning that a transition without adequate capabilities could undermine defensive strength.\n\nThe problem is that the United States has now deemed some items it previously evaluated as \"met\" last year to be \"unmet\" this year. This explains complaints from the South Korean military that the United States is \"moving the goalposts.\" According to reports, the U.S. position is that previously met items must be reassessed annually as the battlefield environment evolves. The U.S. is particularly concerned with surveillance and reconnaissance assets and command-and-control systems. The demands reportedly include improving data links and communication security systems to operate in conjunction with Maven, an artificial intelligence-based targeting analysis system used in actual operations, and ensuring the future combined command headquarters to be led by a South Korean general possesses corresponding capabilities.\n\nOn the surface, this represents how rapid advances in AI utilization and new weapons systems in military operations have emerged as a wild card in OPCON negotiations. However, this is precisely the kind of issue that can be resolved through close coordination at the working level. The reason OPCON transition, a matter on which both nations have already set timelines, is encountering such friction must be soberly examined: there is a fundamental erosion of mutual trust between the two sides. President Lee Jae-myung stated in May that \"even if OPCON is recovered tomorrow, there would be no problem,\" and the Defense Ministry has maintained that there are no major disagreements between Seoul and Washington. However, if FOC verification cannot even be completed, the transition cannot be accelerated through confidence alone. There are observations that Seoul is delaying the Korea-U.S. Security Consultative Meeting schedule to secure time to persuade Washington and is seeking a \"top-down\" resolution between leaders through political agreement.\n\nRather than rushing toward political accord, priority should be given to narrowing disagreements between military authorities regarding evaluation criteria and transition conditions. OPCON transition is a task that must be accomplished for autonomous defense. However, if it is not backed by the establishment of operational capabilities for our military and close communication between Seoul and Washington, public security concerns will only grow.",
