        "body": "Ambassador to US identifies abnormality in intelligence sharing as major challenge Unification Minister Chung Dong-young told reporters at Government Complex Seoul on Monday that there is no problem with the South Korea-US alliance amid controversy over Washington’s restrictions on sharing intelligence with Seoul. Chung rejected Washington’s assessment that his remarks about nuclear facilities in North Korea's Kusong, North Pyongan Province, had disclosed classified US intelligence, calling it a “misunderstanding” and insisting that “the US is wrong.” At a National Assembly audit in Washington on the same day, South Korean Ambassador to the United States Kang Kyung-wha said high-level consultations between the two countries were taking place so infrequently that the situation was frustrating. “I acknowledge that there are limits to communication with the White House,” she added. Asked about the most difficult outstanding issues in bilateral relations, Kang also identified “intelligence cooperation” as a major challenge. At a Cabinet meeting on Tuesday, President Lee Jae Myung said the South Korea-US alliance is very important, but added that submissive and subservient attitudes should be avoided in relations between countries. He also stressed that South Korea is a sovereign, independent nation and that managing relations with the major powers surrounding it was important. No US ally is immune to friction with the Donald Trump administration over security and trade. Yet most allied leaders seek to resolve disputes through behind-the-scenes negotiations, mindful of Washington’s influence. Lee’s remarks about the need to maintain national dignity are perfectly reasonable in principle. But going out of one’s way to declare that South Korea will not submit is diplomatically unwise. The question now is how Washington will interpret his words. During the Korean War, the United States deployed nearly 1.8 million troops to the peninsula, more than 36,000 of whom were killed in action. Today, more than 28,000 US troops remain stationed in South Korea, helping maintain stability and peace on the Korean Peninsula. South Korea also relies heavily on US extended deterrence, including the nuclear umbrella, to counter the threat posed by North Korea’s nuclear arsenal. Against this backdrop, putting the United States on the same footing as neighboring powers such as China and Russia is simply untenable. Lee’s remarks appear to reinforce Chung’s assertion that there is nothing wrong with the South Korea-US alliance. But do the president and the unification minister fully appreciate the alliance’s importance? After Chung exposed the existence of nuclear facilities in Kusong in March, Washington, angered by what it regarded as a leak of classified intelligence about the site, began restricting some intelligence on North Korea gathered by its satellites. The US military monitors North Korea’s nuclear activities through hundreds of reconnaissance satellites and other surveillance assets. The imagery and signals intelligence it collects are vital to South Korea’s ability to counter the threat from a nuclear-armed North Korea, especially given that Seoul has no nuclear weapons of its own. Any curtailment of such intelligence sharing is therefore a matter of grave concern. Above all, intelligence and military cooperation depend on mutual trust. Restrictions on intelligence sharing send a troubling signal that confidence between the two sides has been eroded. The two countries also face a host of major unresolved issues, including tariffs, South Korean investment in the United States and nuclear energy cooperation. If Seoul cannot maintain effective communication with key decision-makers in the White House, its national interests risk being pushed aside. The US alliance remains a lifeline for South Korea. The stark reality is that Seoul still has much to seek from Washington on a range of issues. It cannot afford to let rifts in the alliance deepen and then pay the price in both security and economic terms. The government must act swiftly to restore trust and resolve the intelligence-sharing dispute through concrete confidence-building measures. This should include reviewing personnel arrangements where necessary to address US concerns. Reassuring Washington that Seoul takes the alliance seriously is not a matter of submission; it is a matter of protecting South Korea’s national interests. Korea's GDP per capita to top $40,000 this year, $50,000 in 2028, IMF says South Korea's gross domestic product per capita is projected to exceed $40,000 for the first time this year, according to the International Monetary Fund. Hyundai nears 1 million vehicle capacity in India with new Pune plant [Exclusive] Gangnam goes all out to attract AMD AI research center Shoppers, staff describe fight to stop Incheon knife attack Yoo Hae-jin was Korea's favorite everyman. Then came 'The Assassin(s)' When freelance teachers count as workers Survive & Thrive US rules give Korean LFP makers rare opening against China China Factor K-pop Inside Out Uncovering the stories behind K-pop’s latest moves True Crime Take a stroll through the darker side of Korea’s past Traditional Market Voyage The Korea Herald’s guide to the markets that dot Korea’s capital Herald Interview A series of in-depth interviews. [Lee Byung-jong] Coalition of middle powers [Editorial] Restoring trust [Editorial] Shift in tides [Editorial] CPTPP bid in limbo [Shang-Jin Wei] Why US-China trade matters Father, 4-year-old daughter from Australia found dead on Jeju amid custody dispute Woman in her 20s dies after collapsing during Seoul Race half-marathon Man with electronic monitoring device allegedly stabs 8 in Incheon Homeplus Hyundai bets $15.5b on India as global production hub Korea's GDP per capita to top $40,000 this year, IMF says",
